namespace gismo {
/**

\page ieti_AF_example ieti_AF_example.cpp

This example extends \ref ieti_example by implementing the
**IETI-AF** (**All-Floating** IETI, also known as *Total FETI*) variant
alongside the standard **IETI-DP** solver. Both methods solve the same
Poisson problem on the same multi-patch isogeometric discretization; the
two solutions are then compared to verify that they are numerically
equivalent.

\tableofcontents

---

\section ieti_AF_problem Problem statement

We solve the Poisson boundary value problem
\f{align*}{
    -\Delta u &= f    &&\text{in } \Omega, \\
            u &= g_D  &&\text{on } \Gamma_D, \\
  \partial_n u &= g_N  &&\text{on } \Gamma_N,
\f}
on a non-overlapping multi-patch domain
\f[
   \overline\Omega = \bigcup_{k=1}^K \overline{\Omega_k},
   \quad \Omega_k \cap \Omega_l = \emptyset \text{ for } k\neq l.
\f]
On each patch we have an isogeometric function space \f$V_k\f$.
We assume *fully matching* interfaces, so the global space
\f$V_h = \{v : v|_{\Omega_k}\in V_k\}\f$ is conforming.

Patch-wise assembly gives local stiffness matrices \f$A_k\f$ and load
vectors \f$\underline f_k\f$ for \f$k=1,\ldots,K\f$.

---

\section ieti_AF_dp_vs_af IETI-DP vs. IETI-AF

Both methods solve the *same* saddle-point problem using Lagrange
multipliers that enforce the continuity constraints between patches.
They differ in how the Dirichlet boundary conditions are treated.

| Feature | IETI-DP | IETI-AF |
|---|---|---|
| Dirichlet BCs | Eliminated from local matrices | Enforced by extra Lagrange multipliers |
| Dirichlet dofs | Removed before assembly | Kept as free dofs; constrained by additional \f$\lambda\f$ |
| Every patch | May be *pinned* by Dirichlet BCs | All patches are *floating* subdomains |
| Local null space | Eliminated by Dirichlet BCs | Constants kernel present on every patch |
| Jump matrix \f$B_k\f$ | Interface rows only | Interface rows **plus** Dirichlet constraint rows |
| Coarse problem | Directly SPD | Singular until Dirichlet applied at coarse level |

The name *All-Floating* refers to the fact that the Dirichlet boundary
conditions no longer eliminate degrees of freedom from individual
patches; every patch is a *floating* subdomain whose local stiffness
matrix \f$A_k\f$ is singular (its null space contains the constant
function). The constants are ultimately killed through the combination
of primal corner constraints and Dirichlet Lagrange multipliers.

---

\section ieti_AF_math Mathematical formulation

\subsection ieti_AF_interface Interface jump constraints

Let \f$B_k^{\text{int}}\f$ be the interface jump matrix for patch
\f$k\f$ (assembled by `gsIetiMapper::computeJumpMatrices()`). In both
IETI-DP and IETI-AF we impose
\f[
   \sum_{k=1}^K B_k^{\text{int}} \underline u_k = 0
   \quad \text{(continuity at interfaces)}.
\f]

\subsection ieti_AF_dirichlet Dirichlet Lagrange multipliers

In IETI-AF we **do not** eliminate the Dirichlet dofs. Instead, for
every unique global Dirichlet dof \f$i\f$ (identified via a reference
`gsDofMapper` with Dirichlet elimination), we add **one** extra
Lagrange multiplier enforcing
\f[
   u_{k(i),i} = g_D(x_i),
\f]
where \f$k(i)\f$ is the first patch that carries dof \f$i\f$. Because
shared boundary dofs are already linked across patches by the interface
Lagrange multipliers, creating a separate Lagrange multiplier per patch
for the same physical point would make the system rank-deficient.

Collecting all Dirichlet rows into a matrix
\f$B^{\text{dir}}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{n_D \times n_k}\f$
(with one +1 per row), the **augmented jump matrix** for patch \f$k\f$
is
\f[
   B_k^{\text{aug}}
   =
   \begin{pmatrix}
       B_k^{\text{int}} \\[3pt]
       B_k^{\text{dir}}
   \end{pmatrix}
   \in \mathbb{R}^{(n_\lambda^{\text{int}}+n_D)\times n_k}.
\f]
The augmented constraint reads
\f[
   \sum_{k=1}^K B_k^{\text{aug}} \underline u_k
   =
   \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \underline d \end{pmatrix},
\f]
where \f$\underline d \in \mathbb{R}^{n_D}\f$ collects the Dirichlet
values \f$g_D(x_i)\f$.

\subsection ieti_AF_saddlepoint IETI saddle-point system

Using the augmented jump matrices and local saddle-point blocks
(introduced by the primal system; see \ref ieti_example for details),
the IETI-AF saddle-point system is
\f[
    \begin{pmatrix}
       \tilde A_1 &            &             &                &  (\tilde B_1^{\text{aug}})^\top \\
                  & \tilde A_2 &             &                &  (\tilde B_2^{\text{aug}})^\top \\
                  &            &   \ddots    &                &  \vdots              \\
                  &            &             & \tilde A_{K+1} &  (\tilde B_{K+1}^{\text{aug}})^\top \\
       \tilde B_1^{\text{aug}} & \tilde B_2^{\text{aug}} &   \cdots    & \tilde B_{K+1}^{\text{aug}} &     0 \\
    \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \underline{\tilde u}_1 \\
        \vdots \\
        \underline{\tilde u}_{K+1} \\
        \underline\lambda
    \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \underline{\tilde f}_1 \\
        \vdots \\
        \underline{\tilde f}_{K+1} \\
        \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \underline d \end{pmatrix}
    \end{pmatrix}.
\f]
The Schur complement form is
\f[
    F^{\text{aug}} \underline\lambda
    = \underline g^{\text{aug}} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \underline d \end{pmatrix},
\f]
where
\f[
    F^{\text{aug}} = \sum_{k=1}^{K+1} \tilde B_k^{\text{aug}}
                     \tilde A_k^{-1} (\tilde B_k^{\text{aug}})^\top,
    \qquad
    \underline g^{\text{aug}} = \sum_{k=1}^{K+1} \tilde B_k^{\text{aug}}
                     \tilde A_k^{-1} \underline{\tilde f}_k.
\f]

\subsection ieti_AF_coarse Singularity of the primal coarse matrix

In IETI-DP, the primal coarse matrix
\f$P = \tilde A_{K+1} = \sum_k \Psi_k^\top A_k \Psi_k\f$
is a discrete energy inner product on the corner dofs and is regular
(symmetric positive definite) because every corner dof that is not
shared is pinned by Dirichlet elimination.

In IETI-AF, Dirichlet elimination is **not** applied to the local
matrices before `gsPrimalSystem::handleConstraints()`. Consequently
\f$P\f$ inherits the kernel of the original local problems: if none of
the corner primals lies on the Dirichlet boundary (which happens, for
example, in the 2-patch unit square), \f$P\f$ is a pure Laplacian on
corner values and admits the global constant as a null vector.

The fix is to apply a **coarse-level Dirichlet elimination**: for every
primal dof \f$i_d\f$ that corresponds to a Dirichlet corner, we set
\f[
   P_{i_d,:} = P_{:,i_d} = \delta_{i_d}, \quad
   (\underline f_P)_{i_d} = g_D(x_{i_d}),
\f]
and adjust the constraint RHS by
\f[
   \Delta \underline g = B_P^{\text{aug}} \underline d_P,
\f]
where \f$\underline d_P\f$ holds the Dirichlet values for the pinned
primal dofs and \f$B_P^{\text{aug}}\f$ is the (now zero-column-cleared)
primal jump matrix. The complete corrected Schur RHS is therefore
\f[
    \text{rhs}^{\text{Schur}}
    = \underline g^{\text{aug}}
    - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \underline d \end{pmatrix}
    + \Delta \underline g.
\f]

\subsection ieti_AF_prec Preconditioner

The interface block of \f$F^{\text{aug}}\f$ is preconditioned with the
standard scaled Dirichlet preconditioner
\f[
    M_{sD} = \sum_{k=1}^K \hat B_k^{\text{int}} D_k^{-1} S_k D_k^{-1}
             (\hat B_k^{\text{int}})^\top,
\f]
where \f$S_k\f$ is the Schur complement of the *original* (not
augmented) \f$A_k\f$ with respect to the interface dofs and
\f$D_k\f$ is the multiplicity scaling matrix.

The Dirichlet block (bottom-right of \f$F^{\text{aug}}\f$) is
preconditioned by an **identity operator**, giving the full
block-diagonal preconditioner
\f[
    M^{\text{AF}} = \begin{pmatrix} M_{sD} & 0 \\ 0 & I_{n_D} \end{pmatrix}.
\f]

---

\section ieti_AF_impl Implementation walk-through

The implementation is split between the helper functions `solveIetiAF()`
and `solveIetiDP()` and the `main()` function.

\subsection ieti_AF_impl_ref Reference mapper (step A)

We first build a `gsDofMapper` **with** Dirichlet elimination to
identify which patch-local dof indices correspond to Dirichlet
nodes and to retrieve their interpolated values:

```cpp
gsExprAssembler<> assembler;
space u = assembler.getSpace(mb);
gsBoundaryConditions<> bcCopy = bc;
bcCopy.setGeoMap(mp);
u.setup(bcCopy, dirichlet::interpolation, 0);
mapperDir    = u.mapper();
fixedPartDir = u.fixedPart();
```

\subsection ieti_AF_impl_ieti IETI mapper without Dirichlet (step B)

The `gsIetiMapper` is initialised with an **empty** boundary-condition
object so that every basis function remains a free dof:

```cpp
gsExprAssembler<> assembler;
space u = assembler.getSpace(mb);
gsBoundaryConditions<> bcEmpty;
u.setup(bcEmpty, dirichlet::interpolation, 0);
ietiMapper.init(mb, u.mapper(), u.fixedPart());
```

\subsection ieti_AF_impl_corners Corner primals (step B, continued)

Because every patch is floating, every patch must have at least one
primal constraint to kill its local constant null space. We therefore
register **all** corners as primals — including corners that sit on the
Dirichlet boundary (which `gsIetiMapper::cornersAsPrimals()` would
skip). We use `customPrimalConstraints()` and group corners that share
the same global dof into one primal constraint:

```cpp
for (boxCorner it = ...; it != ...; ++it) {
    const index_t globalIdx = ietiMapper.dofMapperGlobal().index(idx, k);
    // store (patch_index, unit-vector constraint) in groups[globalIdx]
    // and remember whether this corner is Dirichlet
}
for (auto& kv : groups) {
    const index_t primalIdx = ietiMapper.nPrimalDofs();
    ietiMapper.customPrimalConstraints(kv.second.first);
    if (kv.second.second.first)   // Dirichlet corner
        dirichletPrimals.push_back({primalIdx, kv.second.second.second});
}
```

\subsection ieti_AF_impl_dirlm Dirichlet Lagrange multipliers (step C)

We iterate over every unique global Dirichlet dof (across all patches)
and create exactly one extra Lagrange multiplier for each, recording
its local dof index in the IETI-AF mapper:

```cpp
std::set<index_t> seenGlobalDir;
for (index_t k = 0; k < nPatches; ++k)
    for (index_t i = 0; i < mapperDir.patchSize(k); ++i) {
        const index_t globalDir = mapperDir.index(i, k);
        if (!mapperDir.is_boundary_index(globalDir)) continue;
        if (!seenGlobalDir.insert(globalDir).second) continue;  // deduplicate
        // record (extraLM_index, local_dof_index) for patch k
        dirichletRows[k].push_back({extraLM, localIdx});
        dirichletValsVec.push_back(val);
        ++extraLM;
    }
```

\subsection ieti_AF_impl_assembly Per-patch assembly (step D)

Local stiffness is assembled **without** Dirichlet elimination.
Only Neumann conditions are passed to the local assembler; Dirichlet
values are handled entirely through the Lagrange multipliers.

After assembly, the augmented jump matrix is formed by stacking the
interface rows (from `ietiMapper.jumpMatrix(k)`) above the Dirichlet
rows:

```cpp
gsSparseEntries<> entries;
// copy interface rows
for (...) entries.add(row, col, val);
// append Dirichlet rows
for (auto& pr : dirichletRows[k])
    entries.add(nInterfaceLM + pr.first, pr.second, 1.0);
gsSparseMatrix<real_t, RowMajor> jumpAug(nTotalLM, nLocal);
jumpAug.setFrom(entries);
```

The scaled Dirichlet preconditioner is fed **only** the interface jump
matrix (it works on the interface skeleton dofs and is unaware of the
Dirichlet LM block).

\subsection ieti_AF_impl_coarseDir Coarse-level Dirichlet elimination (step E)

After `primal.handleConstraints()` is called for all patches, the
primal local matrix \f$P\f$ may be singular. We pin every primal dof
that corresponds to a Dirichlet corner:

```cpp
// 1. subtract column-sweep contribution from rhsP
for (index_t j = 0; j < P.outerSize(); ++j) {
    if (!isDir[j]) continue;
    for (InnerIterator it(P, j); it; ++it)
        if (!isDir[it.row()])
            rhsP(it.row(), 0) -= it.value() * dirVal(j, 0);
}
// 2. replace Dirichlet rows/cols by identity
// 3. set Dirichlet rhs
for (auto& pr : dirichletPrimals)
    rhsP(pr.first, 0) = pr.second;
// 4. accumulate correction to global Schur RHS
coarseSchurAdj += Bp * dirVal;
// 5. zero Dirichlet columns of Bp
```

\subsection ieti_AF_impl_schurrhs Corrected Schur RHS (step F)

The Schur RHS assembled by `gsIetiSystem::rhsForSchurComplement()`
is \f$\underline g^{\text{aug}}\f$. We subtract the Dirichlet value
vector and add the coarse correction:

```cpp
gsMatrix<> rhsForSchur = ieti.rhsForSchurComplement();
rhsForSchur.bottomRows(nDirichletLM) -= dirichletVals;
rhsForSchur                          += coarseSchurAdj;
```

\subsection ieti_AF_impl_solve PCG solve (step G)

The block-diagonal preconditioner is assembled and CG is run on the
corrected Schur system. The solution is reconstructed per patch
exactly as in IETI-DP:

```cpp
gsBlockOp<>::Ptr bp = gsBlockOp<>::make(2, 2);
bp->addOperator(0, 0, sdPrec);                       // scaled Dirichlet
bp->addOperator(1, 1, gsIdentityOp<>::make(nDirLM)); // identity for Dir LMs
gsConjugateGradient<> PCG(ieti.schurComplement(), bp);
PCG.setOptions(solverOpts).solveDetailed(rhsForSchur, lambda, errorHistory);
```

---

\section ieti_AF_usage Usage

```
ieti_AF_example [options]

  -g  <file>   Geometry file (default: domain2d/yeti_mp2.xml)
      --SplitPatches <n>  Uniformly split every patch n times (default: 1)
      --StretchGeometry <s>  Stretch in x-direction (default: 1)
  -r  <n>      Number of uniform h-refinements (default: 1)
  -p  <d>      Spline degree (default: 2)
  -b  <str>    Boundary conditions per boundary: 'd'=Dirichlet, 'n'=Neumann
               A single character applies to all boundaries (default: "d")
  -c  <str>    Primal constraints: 'c'=corners, 'e'=edge averages,
               'f'=face averages (default: "c")
  -e           Eliminate pointwise primal constraints (corners) from local sys.
  -t  <tol>    CG stopping tolerance (default: 1e-8)
      --Solver.MaxIterations <n>  (default: 100)
      --plot   Write ParaView output files
```

\subsection ieti_AF_usage_example Example runs

```bash
# Default: yeti geometry, degree 2, 1 refinement, corners as primals
./build/bin/ieti_AF_example

# Higher resolution with edge primals
./build/bin/ieti_AF_example -r 3 -p 3 -c ce

# Pure Neumann problem (all patches floating, no Dirichlet at all)
./build/bin/ieti_AF_example -b n

# Paraview output
./build/bin/ieti_AF_example --plot
```

\subsection ieti_AF_usage_output Expected output

```
*** Running IETI-DP ***
IETI-DP: 13 iterations, final residual 2.90777e-09

*** Running IETI-AF (Total FETI) ***
IETI-AF: 35 iterations, final residual 5.22744e-09

*** Comparison ***
max |coef_DP - coef_AF| over all patches : ...
relative max-coefficient difference      : ...
||u_DP - u_AF||_L2  : ...
relative L2 diff    : 6.40242e-10

OK: IETI-AF matches IETI-DP within 1e-06
```

IETI-AF typically needs more CG iterations than IETI-DP because the
Dirichlet LM block uses only an identity preconditioner, but the
two methods converge to the same discrete solution.

---

